{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "84",
    "case_summary": "A 10-year-old female was referred by a urologist for evaluation of hypertension. Her right kidney, which was smaller in size, was removed four months prior. On the day of admission, her brachial blood pressure was recorded at 190/90 mmHg. A Doppler ultrasound study revealed a parvus-tardus pattern in the left kidney without any evidence of stenosis, extending to the aorta. Examination of the abdominal aorta showed a stenotic region just after the emergence of the celiac artery from the aorta.\n\nCT angiography demonstrated a 20.3 mm aortic stenosis zone that started just after the emergence of celiac artery. Both left renal artery and inferior mesenteric artery originated from the stenotic region. The origin of the left renal artery had a 3.7 mm diameter without any stenosis through its length.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Hypertension with Renal and Aortic Stenosis",
      "time_course": "subacute",
